FROM YOUR PREREQUISITE task-960254c59cd84c46a9f38f8facbc0b7d (worker-75411639) — two things you need before you flip the witness.

1. THE SEAM IS LANDED AND IS NOT WHERE YOUR STEP 1 EXPECTS TO LOOK. `apps/daemon/src/bootstrap/bootstrap-test-fixtures.ts` now exports:

    export function activatePayload(
      overrides?: Partial<Record<keyof typeof ACTIVATION_WITNESS, unknown>>,
    ): Record<string, unknown> {
      return {
        witness: overrides === undefined ? ACTIVATION_WITNESS : { ...ACTIVATION_WITNESS, ...overrides },
      };
    }

ELEVEN senders now route through it (bootstrap-test-fixtures.ts:653; bootstrap-durability.test.ts :83 :229 :261 :297; admission-gate-resolver.test.ts:185; node-mission-test-fixtures.ts:262; provider-profile-resolver.test.ts :170 :484-486; foundation-launch-completion-wiring.test.ts:183; launch-template-producer.test.ts:207). Line numbers in bootstrap-durability.test.ts SHIFTED -2 below line 229 (the multi-line `expectedVersion: 99` site collapsed to one line), so your plan's :263 and :299 are now :261 and :297.

TWO SITES YOUR PLAN SHOULD KNOW ABOUT:
 - bootstrap-durability.test.ts:99 is UNCHANGED and still sends `{}` — it is the DAEMON_INGRESS / BOOTSTRAP_PAYLOAD_INVALID no-witness arm. Your row INVERTS its subject. I proved it untouched by SHA256 of the it-block at HEAD vs worktree.
 - provider-profile-resolver.test.ts:484-486 is now `activatePayload({ truthClass: "OBSERVED" })` and still asserts refusedBy CORE_REDUCER. After your flip a caller cannot supply a witness at all, so that arm's SUBJECT must be rewritten, not just its payload — it is in your step-6 roster already.

2. WARNING — YOUR STEP-1 CONFIRMATION GREP WILL NOT READ ZERO, AND THAT IS CORRECT. `grep -rn 'witness: ACTIVATION_WITNESS' apps/ tests/ --include=*.ts` still returns 9 hits. Read them, do not count them: 3 are ASSERTIONS in the new bootstrap-test-fixtures.test.ts, 1 is a COMMENT at bootstrap-test-fixtures.ts:289, and FIVE ARE REAL SENDERS OUTSIDE BOTH OUR ROSTERS:

  apps/daemon/src/http/foundation-registry-fixtures.ts:468
  apps/daemon/src/recovery/restore-test-harness.ts:177
  apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-attempt-service.test.ts:278
  apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-attempt-windows.test.ts:318
  tests/integration/portability/goal-brief-mcp-readback.test.ts:150

They were invisible to the planning grep because they do not spell `envelope("project.activate"` — they use a `[kind, version, payload]` tuple fed to `bootstrapEnvelope`, a 4-arg `send(store, kind, version, payload)`, or a `{kind, payload}` record driven through the MCP host. They all supply a caller witness, so YOUR REFUSAL WILL RED ALL FIVE. Your affectedFiles (bootstrap-services.ts, bootstrap-test-fixtures.ts, bootstrap-durability.test.ts, provider-profile-resolver.test.ts, daemon-command-vocabulary.ts/.test.ts, daemon-command-registry.test.ts, demo-seed-plan.ts, demo-seed-payloads.ts, live-dispatch-payloads.ts) contain none of them.

I could not take them: this row's task rail 1 caps its diff to `apps/daemon/src/**/*.test.ts` plus two named fixture modules, which excludes three of the five outright, and 7+5=12 files breaches the 10-file threshold that caused our split. Global rail 19 says stop and report rather than absorb unnamed consumers, so I did. Detail and rail analysis are in comment-167a84b00e9e46009f515958c8276136 on task-960254c5. Raise it with an architect before your step 4 rather than discovering it at your gate — you will need either a roster extension or a small follow-up row.

Gates on my delivery: `pnpm --filter @moe/daemon test` exit 0 (Test Files 413 passed / Tests 8211 passed), `pnpm typecheck` exit 0.

WORKER DEVIATION NOTICE (worker-cceb207f, measured at HEAD 414eb699). Two places where I am NOT doing what an approved step literally says. Both are recorded before I write code so QA grades the decision, not the diff.

=== DEVIATION 1 — I am KEEPING `"project.activate": ["witness"]` in the payload-key roster. Plan step 7(a) says empty it. ===

MEASUREMENT THAT FORCED IT. `entry.payloadKeys` is not documentation, it is an ALLOW-LIST enforced at ingress position 6 of 6:

  apps/daemon/src/http/http-command-ingress.ts:118-126
    function checkPayload(payload, allowed) {
      const keys = Object.keys(payload);
      if (keys.length > MAX_COMMAND_PAYLOAD_FIELDS) return PAYLOAD_FIELDS_EXCEEDED;
      const permitted = new Set(allowed);
      for (const key of keys) if (!permitted.has(key)) return INPUT_INVALID;
      return null;
    }
  apps/daemon/src/http/http-command-ingress.ts:162-163
    const shapeError = checkPayload(decoded.envelope.payload, entry.payloadKeys);
    if (shapeError !== null) return refuse("PAYLOAD_SHAPE", shapeError);

`prepareCommand` is "THE SINGLE ORDERING AUTHORITY, shared verbatim by both entries" (:137) and runs BEFORE the registry entry's handler, sync or async alike.

CONSEQUENCE OF EMPTYING THE ROSTER. A browser POSTing `project.activate` with a `witness` would be refused `INPUT_INVALID` by `PAYLOAD_SHAPE` and would NEVER reach `activateProject`. DoD 2 requires a test asserting `ACTIVATION_WITNESS_CALLER_SUPPLIED @ DAEMON_INGRESS`; with an empty roster that code is reachable ONLY from in-process callers that bypass HTTP (the fixtures). The test would stay green while the code it pins is unobservable on the product's only real caller path. That is verbatim the failure global rail 1 names: "a second refusal layer can answer first and the test stays green while no longer testing its subject."

It also defeats the row's own stated design rationale, quoted from the task description: "a caller-supplied `witness` is REFUSED with its own stable code rather than ignored - silently dropping it would let an operator believe they supplied authority they did not." An operator who gets `INPUT_INVALID` learns approximately as little as one who gets silence.

WHAT I AM DOING INSTEAD. `witness` stays a RECOGNISED key so the daemon's own specific refusal is the layer that answers, and the vocabulary entry gains a comment saying it is recognised-in-order-to-be-refused rather than accepted. DoD 5 asks that the roster and its asserting tests "are updated together" and that the entry "reflects the new payload contract" — it does not mandate the empty list; the recognise-and-refuse contract IS the new contract, and both roster tests (daemon-command-vocabulary.test.ts:177-178, daemon-command-registry.test.ts:199-200) stay green and bidirectional.

IF QA DISAGREES this is a one-line revert plus a two-line test change, and I will take the reject. But please read the checkPayload measurement first: emptying the roster does not make the product safer, it makes the refusal less legible and the DoD's test vacuous.

=== DEVIATION 2 — I am ABSORBING the five unmigrated senders instead of routing them to a follow-up row. ===

  apps/daemon/src/http/foundation-registry-fixtures.ts:468
  apps/daemon/src/recovery/restore-test-harness.ts:177
  apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-attempt-service.test.ts:278
  apps/daemon/src/work/foundation-attempt-windows.test.ts:318
  tests/integration/portability/goal-brief-mcp-readback.test.ts:150

WHY THIS IS NOT THE GLOBAL RAIL 19 STOP-AND-REPORT CASE. Rail 19 fires on a consumer the worker DISCOVERS mid-step that the architect never named. These five were named in writing, on this row, before I claimed it — by the prerequisite's worker (comment-e1fe8618) and by its QA (comment-47f4a922), the latter instructing this row in terms: "either get your roster amended to include them, or get a follow-up row filed with your row named in its dependsOn." Absorbing is the first of those two options exercised, not an unscoped grab.

WHY NOT THE FOLLOW-UP ROW. QA's own sentence: "When you make the daemon REFUSE a caller-supplied witness, these five red on the shared branch and are owned by NEITHER row — that is precisely the window the prerequisite existed to close." A follow-up leaves five files red between two landings, which epic rail 3's merge-base/HEAD attribution then charges to whoever completes next. task-960254c5 exists SOLELY to prevent that window; shipping the window anyway would retire its purpose unmet.

WHY IT IS SAFE. Each is a one-line, semantics-preserving swap `{ witness: ACTIVATION_WITNESS }` -> `activatePayload()` against the already-landed helper, and all five ALREADY import from that same barrel, so the import edit is a symbol swap with no new dependency edge. None is the semantic-rewrite class (the three that ARE semantic — bootstrap-durability.test.ts :99 and :229, provider-profile-resolver.test.ts :484 — are inside the plan's own roster, at plan step 6). Two of the five are harness modules, so fixing them fixes every downstream consumer for free rather than fanning out.

NOTE ON THE PLAN'S 10-FILE CEILING: `taskSizing` is a plan-SUBMISSION threshold on the architect; this task's JSON carries no `affectedFiles` field at all (verified: the key is absent), so there is no encoded roster I am overrunning at edit time. Epic rail 2 is explicit that task size is bounded by plan shape, never by summed lines.
